<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Liu Song</h1><p class="st-meta">420 CE – 479 CE · capital Jiankang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first of the Southern Dynasties, founded by the general Liu Yu, whose Yuanjia era was a southern golden age before family murders tore the dynasty apart.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a>, a commander of humble origins, defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/huan-chu">Huan Chu</a>, the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-en">Sun En</a> and the northern states of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-yan">Southern Yan</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a>. In 420 he forced the last <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a> emperor to abdicate and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>. Historians call it Liu Song to distinguish it from the later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-liu-song">Liu Yu</a> relied on military men and curbed the great aristocratic clans. Under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-liu-song">Emperor Wen</a>, the Yuanjia era (424 to 453) brought prosperity and cultural achievement, including Fan Ye's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">History of the Later Han</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-lingyun">Xie Lingyun's</a> landscape poetry; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zu-chongzhi">Zu Chongzhi's</a> calendar followed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowu-of-liu-song">Emperor Xiaowu</a>. A northern offensive in 450 provoked a <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> counterattack to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-liu-song">Emperor Wen</a> was murdered by his heir in 453, and later rulers, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-ziye">Liu Ziye</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-ming-of-liu-song">Emperor Ming</a>, massacred their own relatives. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiao-daocheng">Xiao Daocheng</a> gained control and in 479 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shun-of-liu-song">Emperor Shun</a> to abdicate, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-qi">Southern Qi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/liu-song.jpg" alt="Liu Song" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_and_Southern_Dynasties_1.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>420 CE – 479 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiankang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Emperor Wu of Liu Song, Emperor Wen of Liu Song, Emperor Shun of Liu Song</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Song (Southern Dynasties), Former Song</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=450&amp;focus=liu-song" aria-label="Liu Song on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7737 1011 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Liu Song lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8057 1267L7855 1179M8057 1267L8057 1267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8057 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8057 1267h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jiankang. <a href="/atlas?year=450&amp;focus=liu-song">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
